Hello,

The landlord has a right to enter your unit for the following reasons:
Landlord's right to enter rental unit
54(1)   A landlord shall not enter a rental unit occupied by a tenant under=
 a tenancy agreement except where
(a) an emergency exists and entry to the rental unit is necessary;
(b) the tenant consents to the entry;
(c) the landlord shows the rental unit to a prospective tenant after notice=
 of termination is given under this Act but before the tenant has vacated t=
he unit;
(d) the landlord inspects the rental unit on the day the tenant is required=
 to vacate the unit to complete a rental unit condition report or to determ=
ine if the tenant has fulfilled the tenant's obligations under this Act and=
 the tenancy agreement, and the inspection takes place at a reasonable time;
(e) the landlord in good faith believes that the rental unit has been aband=
oned by the tenant and gives written notice to the tenant in accordance wit=
h subsection (2);
(f) the landlord gives written notice in accordance with subsection (3) and=
 entry is for one of the following purposes:
(i) to perform an obligation of the landlord under this Act or the tenancy =
agreement or to inspect the rental unit to determine if an obligation shoul=
d be performed,
(ii) to complete a rental unit condition report if the tenant has requested=
 the landlord's consent to an assignment or subletting,
(iii) to permit a mortgagee or insurer or prospective mortgagee or insurer =
to inspect the unit when a mortgage or insurance coverage is being arranged=
 or renewed on the residential complex, or
(iv) to show the rental unit to prospective purchasers of the rental unit o=
r residential complex;
(g) the landlord enters the rental unit to provide tenant services to the t=
enant in accordance with the tenancy agreement.


You will notice that renovations is not listed in that section of the Act. =
There is a separate process for doing extensive renovations and that proces=
s involves terminating your tenancy where you would have to move out comple=
tely so that they can do the renovations. If you wanted to move back in aft=
er those renovations are completed you would have the right of first refusa=
l. The question becomes, is this an extensive renovation? If it would mean =
that you would be without water and maybe even electricity for some extende=
d period of time, it might qualify as that.

If you refuse them entry to do this they may give you a warning letter stat=
ing that you cannot refuse entry. If you continue to refuse entry after tha=
t they could give you an eviction notice. If that happens you have a right =
to dispute the notice and not move. They would more than likely contact us =
to schedule a hearing about the eviction and at this hearing we would then =
make a decision on whether you had a right to refuse and whether this was s=
omething the landlord had a right to do.

We can also try to reach out to the landlord and ask them some questions ab=
out this and determine whether or not they can do this now or after termina=
ting a tenancy. If you would like us to contact them please provide a conta=
ct and phone number for your landlord.

Thanks.

Rtb/lg




