<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"mso-margin-top-alt:auto;mso-margin-bottom-a=
lt:auto"><span style=3D"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,sans=
-serif;color:black">You will notice that renovations is not listed in that =
section of the Act. There is a separate process
 for doing extensive renovations and that process involves terminating your=
 tenancy where you would have to move out completely so that they can do th=
e renovations. If you wanted to move back in after those renovations are co=
mpleted you would have the right
 of first refusal. The question becomes, is this an extensive renovation? I=
f it would mean that you would be without water and maybe even electricity =
for some extended period of time, it might qualify as that.</span><o:p></o:=
p></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"mso-margin-top-alt:auto;mso-margin-bottom-a=
lt:auto"><span style=3D"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,sans=
-serif;color:black">&nbsp;</span><o:p></o:p></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal" style=3D"mso-margin-top-alt:auto;mso-margin-bottom-a=
lt:auto"><span style=3D"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;,sans=
-serif;color:black">If you refuse them entry to do this they may give you a=
 warning letter stating that you cannot refuse entry.
 If you continue to refuse entry after that they could give you an eviction=
 notice. If that happens you have a right to dispute the notice and not mov=
